{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 17, :comments ["<b>And it was when Pharaoh sent the people.</b> To include the mixed multitude and the Cretans whom he sent with them." "<b>And Elohim did not lead.</b> To go to the land of Canaan that He gave them. The vav of <b>ולא נחם</b> is superfluous." "<b>The way of the land of the Philistines, though it is near.</b> Even though it is near — like כי לי כל הארץ, רפאה נפשי כי חטאתי לך, שָׁכַל אֶת־כָּל־יָדָיו כִּי מְנַשֶּׁה הַבְּכוֹר — that is to say, even though the land of the Philistines intervenes with one boundary between the boundary of the land of Egypt and the boundary of the land of Canaan, as we find with Yitzhak, who went down from the land of Canaan to go to the land of Egypt by way of the land of the Philistines because of the famine. And what is the reason He did not lead them by the way of the land of the Philistines? כי אמר אלהים פן ינחם העם, lest the people reconsider having left Egypt." "<b>For Elohim said: lest the people reconsider.</b> One should not raise difficulties against this verse, for it is revealed and known that it is in His power to lead them even by way of the land of the Philistines and they would not return to Egypt. Rather, against the attribute of judgment, which was prosecuting against them to test them — whether they would return or not — the Holy One, blessed be He, said: thus it is revealed before Me that if they are put to the test, they will not withstand it. And I explained this same model in the pericope of Bereshit regarding ועתה פן ישלח ידו." "<b>They see war.</b> That the Philistines will suppose about us: they are coming to avenge the children of Ephraim whom we killed, because they hurried to leave the Egyptian bondage thirty years too early. As we translate, and it was as when Pharaoh sent — on the seventh day of Passover. And they erred in counting the four hundred years from the time of the covenant between the pieces, but they began only from when Yitzhak was born; and they would wage war against Israel and return Israel to Egypt. Another interpretation: <b>כי קרוב הוא</b> — Avimelekh king of the Philistines is close to Pharaoh king of Egypt, and Avraham swore to him: אם תשקור לי ולניני ולנכדי, as stated in Midrash Tehillim on אויבי חיים עצמו. David said: what He gave to Avimelekh for three generations, He gave to Avraham for seven generations — for Moshe is the seventh to Avraham, and still Avimelekh's grandson is alive. Therefore <b>He did not lead them by the way of the land of the Philistines, lest</b> Israel wage war against them while the oath is still in force. And this is what the liturgical poet composed: and HaShem remembered the covenant of the grandfather of Pharaoh, whom Avraham swore to: אם תשקור לי ולניני ולנכדי — and therefore He turned them away from the people of the Philistines." "<b>Lest they see war.</b> The Egyptians would pursue them, and the Philistines would go out to meet them to return them to their Egyptian kinsmen. Moreover, they know that the Israelites are coming against them, for the land of the Philistines belongs to the land of Israel, as I explained in the perasha of Vayera and in the perasha of Toldot, and as is explicit in the book of Joshua. The war would face them front and back, and by this means <b>and they return to Egypt</b> — they would set their hearts to return to Egypt, as they did even when they tarried in the wilderness."], :book :exodus, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 13, :notes {2 "The examples cited are verses where כי means 'though' or 'although': Exodus 19:5, Psalm 41:5, Genesis 48:17.", 4 "The reference 'as we translate' points to Aramaic Targum tradition (in Targum Sheni to Esther or related midrashic sources) connecting Pharaoh's pursuit on the seventh day of Passover with the Ephraimite exodus. דוגמא in comment [2] introduces scriptural parallels where כי means 'though/although'."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:57:53.720334825Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}